From 0e405b0d5c7ece28d05fba69fe31c76b8c9ec6f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 14:42:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 65/66] Make IL emission linear in a scope's binding count
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The closure rewrite held each lambda's in-scope variables in a List and ran
the two operations a list is worst at against it: Contains, once per parameter
reference in the whole tree, and Remove, once per variable as each block scope
ends. Both are linear scans, so emitting a lambda cost the square of the number
of bindings in it. A script's top level is one lambda, which makes the count of
top-level declarations the term that squares — and machine-generated JavaScript
is little else.

Measured on synthetic top-level function declarations, emission at N = 500 /
1 000 / 2 000 took 797 / 2 981 / 13 865 ms while parse stayed at 2.5 ms and
expression-tree construction at 70 ms. Mandreel is 1 364 top-level function
declarations plus a matching var apiece; MandreelLatency is the score that
measures the resulting pause.

The scope becomes a reference-keyed multiset. A multiset rather than a set
because the list held duplicates and both operations depended on it: a variable
registered by two nested block scopes is added twice and must survive the inner
scope's exit, so a HashSet would have taken a live binding out of scope. The
list is kept for scopes below 32 bindings, because hashing a reference is a
runtime call and most scopes are small — dictionary-only bought Mandreel 3.6x
and cost jQuery, one large IIFE full of small function scopes, about 20%. The
comparison is an explicit ReferenceEquals loop rather than List.Contains, which
resolves through EqualityComparer<T>.Default to a virtual Equals per element.

Emission on 2 000 top-level declarations goes 13 864.5 -> 485.7 ms (28.5x), and
cost per declaration from climbing to flat. On the real corpora, ABBA-
interleaved at process granularity over six pairs: Mandreel 21 307 -> 7 015 ms
(0.329x, every pair between 0.29 and 0.40), PdfJS 0.874x, Typescript 0.894x,
Box2D 0.927x, and the two CodeLoad payloads inside noise in both directions —
they are narrow-scope corpora, so there was nothing there to win.

Adds the two probes this was found with. --compile-profile compiles each Octane
corpus against a control with every outermost function body replaced by {},
which sizes roadmap item 1-1; running it is what showed Mandreel taking 17.7 s
with every body already removed. --compile-scaling splits parse, tree
construction and IL emission and varies declaration count and name length
independently, which is what named the exponent.

BROILER_JS_REWRITER_INDEX_THRESHOLD sets the promotion point so the change can
be A/B'd on one build; any value above a real scope size restores the scan.

Repository suite: 7 630 tests across 13 projects, 0 failures.

